{ignore}
<p><code>{fragment 'name'}</code> marks a part of the page. A normal request renders it in
place, as if the tag were not there - which is what the examples below show. A request that
names it - <code>?orders&amp;padFragment=order-list</code>, or a page whose PHP sets
<code>$padFragmentOnly = 'order-list'</code> - gets that fragment's rendering and nothing
else: the page renders up to the end of the fragment, which is then the whole response, bare
and untidied; the rest of the page does not render. The first fragment of that name to finish
is the one sent.</p>

<p>This is the server side of partial page updates: htmx
(<code>hx-get="?orders&amp;padFragment=order-list"</code>) and
<a href="?tag/ajax"><code>{ajax 'orders', fragment='order-list'}</code></a>. An htmx request
that names no fragment asks for the one called like its target: with
<code>hx-target="#order-list"</code> htmx sends <code>HX-Target: order-list</code>, and a page
with a fragment of that name answers it alone. A page without one renders whole.</p>

<p>Keep conditions inside the fragment rather than around it: a fragment asked for that never
rendered is an error under the strict check, and an empty 404 otherwise. A fragment without a
name, or one that never closes, is an error under the strict check. The page cache never
stores a fragment the PHP chose, since its key is the address.</p>
{/ignore}
